  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/....\nThe main concern for next week remains the building heat as the\nsubtropical ridge gradually shifts westward back over our region\nbeginning Sunday. H5 heights are expected to climb from 588-591dm\non Saturday to 594-596dm Sunday before peaking on Tuesday at\naround 596-597dm. Forecast H5 heights Monday through at least\nWednesday fall close to the 97th percentile of climatology for the\nperiod, but forecast highs remain at least a few degrees below\nrecords. An Extreme Heat Watch remains in effect for Monday-\nWednesday for the south-central Arizona lower deserts and its very\nplausible it may need to go through the rest of the workweek as\nthe ridge is not likely to weaken or move much late next week. The\nlatest NBM forecast temperatures for late next week have also\nbeen trending up, basically keeping highs around 110 degrees. The\nmain question about the heat will be the amount of convection\nthat may form over eastern Arizona potentially bring outflows\ninto the lower deserts and/or debris clouds. Guidance does agree\nsome modest moisture advection is expected early next week, but it\nshould only be enough to help higher terrain areas. As of right\nnow, there are no clear signs the convective potential will be\nenough to greatly affect temperatures early next week, so more\nthan likely the current forecast highs of around 110 degrees will\nhold.",
